Satellite-Enabled AI for Global Human Support: 

A Privacy-Preserving Decision-Support Architecture with Formal Safety Constraints and Conservative Escalation Under Extreme Class Imbalance

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI , KIMI 

Abstract

This thesis presents a formally specified architecture for privacy-preserving mental health decision support that integrates satellite connectivity, edge computing, and multi-model artificial intelligence inference under explicit human clinical oversight. The work addresses a fundamental design problem: how to construct a system that provides useful assistance to underserved populations while maintaining formal guarantees regarding safety, privacy, equity, and human authority. The architecture targets decision support for navigation to appropriate human care rather than autonomous therapeutic intervention.

Three theoretical contributions structure this work. First, we develop a tractable partially observable Markov decision process formulation with a discrete support-need state space and a learned observation model employing Exponential family Principal Component Analysis for belief compression. The computational complexity of belief updates is explicitly bounded as $O(|\mathcal{S}|^2) = O(9)$, and policy optimization is specified through finite-horizon model predictive control. Second, we specify a discrete constraint satisfaction safety layer comprising five checkable constraints—data transmission minimization, privacy budget non-exhaustion, human-override authority, prohibited action space exclusion, and uncertainty-bounded autonomy—alongside a rule-based verifier agent grounded in the multi-agent collusion literature that operates without requiring labeled failure data. Third, and most significantly, we present a decision-theoretic escalation framework that correctly responds to extreme class imbalance inherent in mental health crisis prediction, where positive predictive values for suicide risk models remain between 0.01 and 0.14.

The central theoretical result demonstrates that, given these empirical constraints, conservative default actions—non-escalation or human review—are not design flaws but rather the correct decision-theoretic response to realistic base rates. We formalize this through Bayesian risk updating, cost-sensitive policy optimization, and subgroup robustness auditing. The falsifiable hypothesis is stated as follows: the proposed safety-preserving policy satisfies all five specified safety constraints in greater than 99% of simulated trajectories under a defined threat model while achieving expected cost bounded by a baseline comparator.

All specifications in this work are explicitly theoretical. No clinical efficacy is claimed. No deployment readiness is asserted. All parameter values require prospective validation and structured clinical elicitation before any implementation consideration. The satellite connectivity constraints are grounded in empirical measurements, with explicit acknowledgment that satellite-connected populations may receive only SMS-level functionality for the foreseeable future.

