Chapter 6: Decision-Theoretic Escalation Framework Under Extreme Class Imbalance

6.1 Empirical Grounding

The escalation framework is grounded in the empirical literature on predictive accuracy for suicide risk. The key empirical findings are:

Finding 6.1.1 (PPV Limitations). Meta-analyses demonstrate that pooled PPV for suicide risk instruments is 3.7% (95% CI 2.5%–5.4%). The best machine learning models achieve PPV = 0.14 with sensitivity 0.65.

Finding 6.1.2 (False Negative Distribution). Most suicides occur among those classified as low-risk by prediction models. This is a mathematical consequence of low base rates: even with high sensitivity, the absolute number of missed events among the low-risk group exceeds the number of correctly identified events among the high-risk group.

Finding 6.1.3 (Base Rate Constraint). The base rate of suicide in the general population is approximately 0.001 (10 per 100,000 per year). Even in high-risk populations, the base rate rarely exceeds 0.01.

These findings fundamentally constrain escalation design. A system claiming "crisis detection" with high accuracy is either deluded or dangerous.

6.2 Decision-Theoretic Formulation

Definition 6.2.1 (Escalation Action Space). Let $a \in \{a_{\text{no}}, a_{\text{review}}, a_{\text{escalate}}\}$ be the escalation decision.

Definition 6.2.2 (Expected Cost).

\mathbb{E}[C(a_j) | b_t] = \sum_{k} c_{jk} \, P(s_k | b_t)

where $c_{jk} = C(a_j, s_k)$ is the cost of taking action $a_j$ when the true state is $s_k$.

Definition 6.2.3 (Optimal Policy).

\pi^*(b_t) = \arg\min_j \mathbb{E}[C(a_j) | b_t]

6.3 Cost Matrix Specification

The cost matrix must be elicited from clinical experts. The values specified here are placeholders for illustration only.

Table 6.1: Placeholder Cost Matrix

Action \ State $s_{\text{normal}}$ $s_{\text{elevated}}$ $s_{\text{crisis}}$
$a_{\text{no}}$ 0 5 1000
$a_{\text{review}}$ 2 10 200
$a_{\text{escalate}}$ 30 50 50

Technical Specification 6.3.1 (Cost Elicitation Protocol). For future implementation, costs should be elicited from 5–10 clinicians using structured interviews. The elicitation should employ standard gamble or discrete choice experiment methodology to ensure comparability across clinicians. Inter-rater reliability should be reported as a finding. If clinicians disagree substantially, this is itself a finding: the decision problem is genuinely ambiguous, requiring conservative defaults.

Proposition 6.3.1 (Cost Matrix Uncertainty). The optimal policy $\pi^*(b_t)$ depends entirely on the cost matrix. Without reliable cost elicitation, the "optimal" policy is not just unknown—it is unknowable. The framework should therefore be treated as illustrative until costs can be reliably elicited.

6.4 Bayesian Risk Update

Theorem 6.4.1 (Posterior Probability Under Base Rate Constraint). Given base rate $P(s_{\text{crisis}}) = 0.001$, sensitivity 0.9, and specificity 0.95:

P(s_{\text{crisis}} | \text{positive signal}) = \frac{0.9 \cdot 0.001}{0.9 \cdot 0.001 + 0.05 \cdot 0.999} \approx 0.018

A positive signal implies only 1.8% probability of crisis.

Proof. This is a direct application of Bayes' theorem:

P(s | y) = \frac{P(y | s) \cdot P(s)}{P(y)} = \frac{P(y | s) \cdot P(s)}{P(y | s) \cdot P(s) + P(y | \neg s) \cdot P(\neg s)}

Substituting the given values yields the result. $\square$

Corollary 6.4.1 (Impossibility of High-Precision Detection). Under realistic base rates, no model with sensitivity ≤ 0.9 and specificity ≤ 0.95 can achieve PPV > 0.02. This is not a limitation of current models but a mathematical consequence of the base rate.

6.5 Conservative Default Policy

Definition 6.5.1 (Conservative Default Policy). The default action must be $a_{\text{no}}$ or $a_{\text{review}}$, never $a_{\text{escalate}}$. Escalation requires:

1. Very high posterior probability $P(s_{\text{crisis}} | b_t) > \tau_{\text{escalate}}$ (rarely achievable given base rate), OR
2. Explicit human confirmation, OR
3. Observable indicators of imminent harm (specific plan + means + intent)

Theorem 6.5.1 (Optimality of Conservative Default). Under the placeholder cost matrix and base rate $P(s_{\text{crisis}}) = 0.001$, the optimal default action is $a_{\text{no}}$.

Proof. Compute expected costs:

For $a_{\text{no}}$: $\mathbb{E}[C(a_{\text{no}})] = 0 \cdot 0.949 + 5 \cdot 0.05 + 1000 \cdot 0.001 = 0 + 0.25 + 1 = 1.25$

For $a_{\text{review}}$: $\mathbb{E}[C(a_{\text{review}})] = 2 \cdot 0.949 + 10 \cdot 0.05 + 200 \cdot 0.001 = 1.898 + 0.5 + 0.2 = 2.598$

For $a_{\text{escalate}}$: $\mathbb{E}[C(a_{\text{escalate}})] = 30 \cdot 0.949 + 50 \cdot 0.05 + 50 \cdot 0.001 = 28.47 + 2.5 + 0.05 = 31.02$

Therefore, $a_{\text{no}}$ minimizes expected cost. $\square$

Proposition 6.5.1 (Cost Matrix Sensitivity). The optimality of conservative default depends on the cost ratio $C(s_{\text{crisis}}, a_{\text{no}}) / C(s_{\text{normal}}, a_{\text{escalate}})$. If this ratio is sufficiently large, escalation becomes optimal despite low PPV. However, given that unnecessary escalation causes real harm (resource utilization, participant distress, trust erosion), the ratio required to justify default escalation is unlikely to be achieved in practice.

6.6 Threshold-Based Escalation

Theorem 6.6.1 (Optimal Escalation Threshold). Given the placeholder cost matrix, the optimal threshold for escalation is:

\tau^* = \frac{C(s_{\text{normal}}, a_{\text{escalate}}) - C(s_{\text{normal}}, a_{\text{review}})}{C(s_{\text{crisis}}, a_{\text{review}}) - C(s_{\text{crisis}}, a_{\text{escalate}}) + C(s_{\text{normal}}, a_{\text{escalate}}) - C(s_{\text{normal}}, a_{\text{review}})}

= \frac{30 - 2}{200 - 50 + 30 - 2} = \frac{28}{178} \approx 0.157

Proof. Direct application of Theorem 2.5.1. $\square$

Corollary 6.6.1 (Escalation Threshold vs. Achievable Posterior). The optimal escalation threshold $\tau^* = 0.157$ exceeds the achievable posterior $P(s_{\text{crisis}} | \text{positive signal}) = 0.018$ by a factor of approximately 8.7. Therefore, no model output alone can justify escalation; human confirmation or observable indicators of imminent harm are required.

6.7 Formalized Equity Constraint

Definition 6.7.1 (Subgroup Robustness). Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a set of subgroups defined by language preference, interaction pattern, or geography—not demographic attributes. The equity constraint is:

\max_{g \in \mathcal{G}} \mathbb{E}[C(\pi(b_t)) | g] - \min_{g \in \mathcal{G}} \mathbb{E}[C(\pi(b_t)) | g] \leq \tau_{\text{equity}}

Technical Specification 6.7.1 (Federated Auditing). Subgroup labels are held by a trusted third party (Ethics Board). Fairness metrics are computed without the system accessing group labels, using secure aggregation. This satisfies GDPR Article 9 by avoiding the collection of sensitive demographic data at inference time.

Proposition 6.7.1 (Equity Constraint Sufficiency). The equity constraint is necessary but may not be sufficient. Access equity (who gets satellite connectivity vs. terrestrial) and temporal equity (response time under bandwidth constraints) also require consideration.

