Chapter 7: Privacy Architecture with Explicit Tradeoff Specification

7.1 Privacy Budget Allocation

Definition 7.1.1 (Training-Round Budget). Edge inference consumes zero privacy budget. Budget is allocated to federated training rounds:

\epsilon_{\text{total}} = \sum_{r=1}^{R} \epsilon_r

with adaptive allocation:

\epsilon_r = \epsilon_{\text{base}} \cdot w_{\text{stage}(r)}

where $w_{\text{stage}(r)}$ is a stage-dependent weight that allocates more budget to later training rounds when the model is closer to convergence.

Theorem 7.1.1 (Composition). By Rényi DP composition, the total privacy guarantee is $(\alpha, \sum_r \epsilon_r)$-RDP.

Proof. Direct application of Theorem 2.4.1. $\square$

7.2 The Privacy-Safety Tradeoff

Finding 7.2.1 (DP-FL Degradation). The FedMental replication study demonstrates that DP-FL in mental health contexts causes substantial F1 degradation—up to 27.01% even with $\epsilon = 50$—due to distortion of sparse linguistic markers.

Definition 7.2.1 (Explicit Tradeoff Policy).

If $\epsilon_{\text{total}} \leq 10$ degrades F1 below safety-critical threshold:

· Sacrifice privacy for safety
· Use federated learning without DP, plus contractual/legal protections
· This tradeoff is reported transparently rather than hidden

Proposition 7.2.1 (Tradeoff Transparency Requirement). The privacy-safety tradeoff must be explicitly acknowledged rather than hidden behind mathematical formalism. Reviewers and regulators require transparency about what is sacrificed when privacy guarantees are relaxed.

Technical Specification 7.2.1 (DP Mechanism). For future implementation:

1. Mechanism: Gaussian or Laplace mechanism
2. Sensitivity Analysis: Compute $\ell_2$ sensitivity of model updates
3. Composition Accounting: Use Rényi DP for tight composition bounds
4. Clipping: Apply $\ell_2$-norm clipping with threshold determined by sensitivity analysis

