Chapter 13: Conclusion

This thesis has presented a formally specified architecture for privacy-preserving mental health decision support that integrates satellite connectivity, edge computing, and multi-model AI inference under explicit human clinical oversight. The work addresses a fundamental design problem: how to construct a system that provides useful assistance while maintaining formal guarantees about safety, privacy, and human oversight.

The theoretical contributions are threefold. First, the POMDP formulation with learned observation model and E-PCA belief compression provides a tractable framework for belief tracking under uncertainty. Second, the discrete constraint satisfaction safety layer and rule-based verifier agent provide layered safety guarantees without requiring labeled failure data. Third, and most significantly, the decision-theoretic escalation framework demonstrates that conservative default actions are the correct response to extreme class imbalance—not a design flaw but a mathematical necessity.

The central theoretical result is stated as follows: given base rate $P(s_{\text{crisis}}) = 0.001$ and achievable PPV ≤ 0.14, no model output alone can justify escalation. The optimal escalation threshold under plausible cost assumptions exceeds the achievable posterior by a factor of approximately 8.7. Escalation therefore requires human confirmation or observable indicators of imminent harm.

The falsifiable hypothesis H1—that the safety-preserving policy satisfies all five safety constraints in greater than 99% of simulated trajectories while achieving bounded expected cost—provides a clear criterion for evaluating the framework in future simulation work.

This thesis does not claim clinical efficacy, deployment readiness, or empirical validation. It provides a theoretical foundation upon which future empirical work can build. The next phase of research requires simulation implementation, cost elicitation from clinicians, and regulatory analysis before any deployment consideration.

The central insight of this work is not a novel mathematical derivation but an honest engagement with empirical reality: when the base rate is 0.001 and the best PPV is 0.14, the correct default action is conservatism. A system that acknowledges its limitations and routes people to human care is not admitting defeat—it is making the right decision.

