A.2 Safety Constraint Proofs

A.2.1 Safety Filter Feasibility

Theorem A.2.1. For the five constraints defined in Chapter 4, there exists at least one admissible action for all reachable states.

Proof. We must show that $\mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}(z_t) \neq \emptyset$ for all reachable $z_t$.

Consider the constraints:

1. C₁ (Data transmission): Excludes actions that transmit more than $d_{\max}$ data. At least $u_{\text{wait}}$ transmits zero data.
2. C₂ (Privacy budget): Excludes actions that exceed privacy budget. At inference time, this constraint is vacuous (edge inference consumes zero budget). At training time, the budget allocation ensures non-exhaustion.
3. C₃ (Human override): Either all actions are available (no override) or exactly one action is available (override). In both cases, the set is non-empty.
4. C₄ (Prohibited actions): Excludes exactly three actions, leaving at least three actions available.
5. C₅ (Uncertainty-bounded): Either all actions are available (low uncertainty) or two actions are available (high uncertainty).

The intersection: When no override is issued, $\mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}^{(3)} = \mathcal{U}$, which contains all six actions. After C₄, three actions remain. After C₅, at least two actions remain ($u_{\text{review}}$ and $u_{\text{escalate}}$). After C₁, at least $u_{\text{wait}}$ remains (zero data transmission). Therefore, the intersection contains at least one action: $u_{\text{wait}}$ (when uncertainty is low) or $u_{\text{review}}$ (when uncertainty is high). $\blacksquare$

A.2.2 Safety Preservation Under Policy Composition

Theorem A.2.2. If the verifier accepts the model output and the safety filter projects onto the admissible set, then the executed action satisfies all safety constraints.

Proof. Let $a_{\text{exec}}$ be the executed action. By the pipeline definition:

1. The verifier $V$ evaluates model outputs. If $V = \text{reject}$, the system executes $u_{\text{review}}$ (which is in $\mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}$ by the proof of Theorem A.2.1).
2. If $V = \text{flag}$, the action space is restricted to $\{u_{\text{review}}, u_{\text{wait}}\}$, both of which are admissible.
3. If $V = \text{accept}$, the action is chosen from $\mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}$ by the safety filter projection.

In all cases, $a_{\text{exec}} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{admissible}}$, which is the intersection of all constraint-specific admissible sets. Therefore, $a_{\text{exec}}$ satisfies all safety constraints. $\blacksquare$

