A.3 Escalation Framework Proofs

A.3.1 Optimal Threshold Under Cost Asymmetry

Theorem A.3.1. Given cost matrix $C$ with $C(s_0, a_0) < C(s_0, a_1)$ and $C(s_1, a_1) < C(s_1, a_0)$, the optimal decision rule is:

a^* = \begin{cases} a_1 & \text{if } P(s_1 | b_t) > \tau^* \\ a_0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

where:

\tau^* = \frac{C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)}{C(s_1, a_0) - C(s_1, a_1) + C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)}

Proof. The expected cost of action $a_1$ is:

\mathbb{E}[C(a_1) | b_t] = C(s_0, a_1) P(s_0 | b_t) + C(s_1, a_1) P(s_1 | b_t)

= C(s_0, a_1)(1 - P(s_1 | b_t)) + C(s_1, a_1) P(s_1 | b_t)

= C(s_0, a_1) + (C(s_1, a_1) - C(s_0, a_1)) P(s_1 | b_t)

Similarly:

\mathbb{E}[C(a_0) | b_t] = C(s_0, a_0) + (C(s_1, a_0) - C(s_0, a_0)) P(s_1 | b_t)

Action $a_1$ is optimal when:

C(s_0, a_1) + (C(s_1, a_1) - C(s_0, a_1)) P(s_1 | b_t) \leq C(s_0, a_0) + (C(s_1, a_0) - C(s_0, a_0)) P(s_1 | b_t)

Rearranging:

(C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)) \leq (C(s_1, a_0) - C(s_0, a_0) - C(s_1, a_1) + C(s_0, a_1)) P(s_1 | b_t)

(C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)) \leq (C(s_1, a_0) - C(s_1, a_1) + C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)) P(s_1 | b_t)

P(s_1 | b_t) \geq \frac{C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)}{C(s_1, a_0) - C(s_1, a_1) + C(s_0, a_1) - C(s_0, a_0)} = \tau^*

$\blacksquare$

A.3.2 Conservative Default Optimality

Theorem A.3.2. Under base rate $P(s_{\text{crisis}}) = 0.001$ and placeholder cost matrix:

C = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 5 & 1000 \\ 2 & 10 & 200 \\ 30 & 50 & 50 \end{bmatrix}

the optimal default action is $a_{\text{no}}$.

Proof. Compute the expected cost for each action at the prior belief $b_0 = (0.949, 0.05, 0.001)$:

For $a_{\text{no}}$:
\mathbb{E}[C(a_{\text{no}})] = 0 \cdot 0.949 + 5 \cdot 0.05 + 1000 \cdot 0.001 = 0.25 + 1.0 = 1.25

For $a_{\text{review}}$:
\mathbb{E}[C(a_{\text{review}})] = 2 \cdot 0.949 + 10 \cdot 0.05 + 200 \cdot 0.001 = 1.898 + 0.5 + 0.2 = 2.598

For $a_{\text{escalate}}$:
\mathbb{E}[C(a_{\text{escalate}})] = 30 \cdot 0.949 + 50 \cdot 0.05 + 50 \cdot 0.001 = 28.47 + 2.5 + 0.05 = 31.02

Since $1.25 < 2.598 < 31.02$, the optimal default action is $a_{\text{no}}$. $\blacksquare$

A.3.3 Posterior Computation Under Base Rate Constraint

Theorem A.3.3. For binary classification with base rate $P(s_1) = \pi$, sensitivity $\sigma$, and specificity $\psi$, the PPV is:

\text{PPV} = \frac{\sigma \pi}{\sigma \pi + (1-\psi)(1-\pi)}

Proof. By Bayes' theorem:

P(s_1 | \text{positive}) = \frac{P(\text{positive} | s_1) P(s_1)}{P(\text{positive})}

= \frac{\sigma \pi}{\sigma \pi + (1-\psi)(1-\pi)}

where $P(\text{positive}) = P(\text{positive} | s_1) P(s_1) + P(\text{positive} | s_0) P(s_0) = \sigma \pi + (1-\psi)(1-\pi)$. $\blacksquare$

Corollary A.3.1. For $\pi = 0.001$, $\sigma = 0.9$, $\psi = 0.95$:

\text{PPV} = \frac{0.9 \cdot 0.001}{0.9 \cdot 0.001 + 0.05 \cdot 0.999} = \frac{0.0009}{0.0009 + 0.04995} = \frac{0.0009}{0.05085} \approx 0.0177

A.3.4 Impossibility of High-Precision Detection

Theorem A.3.4. For any classifier with sensitivity $\sigma \leq 0.9$ and specificity $\psi \leq 0.95$, operating on a population with base rate $\pi \leq 0.001$, the PPV is bounded above by $0.018$.

Proof. From Theorem A.3.3:

\text{PPV} = \frac{\sigma \pi}{\sigma \pi + (1-\psi)(1-\pi)} \leq \frac{0.9 \cdot 0.001}{0.9 \cdot 0.001 + 0.05 \cdot 0.999} = \frac{0.0009}{0.05085} \approx 0.0177

The inequality follows because PPV is increasing in $\sigma$ and $\psi$, and decreasing in $(1-\psi)$. Substituting the maximum values yields the upper bound. $\blacksquare$

