A.4 Privacy Guarantees

A.4.1 Rényi Differential Privacy Composition

Theorem A.4.1. If mechanisms $\mathcal{M}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{M}_k$ satisfy $(\alpha, \epsilon_1), \ldots, (\alpha, \epsilon_k)$-RDP respectively, then their composition satisfies $(\alpha, \sum_{i=1}^{k} \epsilon_i)$-RDP.

Proof. For adjacent datasets $D$ and $D'$, the composition $\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{M}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{M}_k)$ satisfies:

D_\alpha(\mathcal{M}(D) \| \mathcal{M}(D')) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} D_\alpha(\mathcal{M}_i(D) \| \mathcal{M}_i(D')) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{k} \epsilon_i

where the equality follows from the chain rule for Rényi divergence, and the inequality follows from the RDP guarantee of each mechanism. $\blacksquare$

A.4.2 Privacy Budget Allocation

Theorem A.4.2. For adaptive allocation $\epsilon_r = \epsilon_{\text{base}} \cdot w_{\text{stage}(r)}$, the total privacy guarantee is $(\alpha, \epsilon_{\text{total}})$-RDP where $\epsilon_{\text{total}} = \epsilon_{\text{base}} \sum_{r=1}^{R} w_{\text{stage}(r)}$.

Proof. Direct application of Theorem A.4.1 with the adaptive allocation. The total budget is the sum of per-round budgets. $\blacksquare$

Proposition A.4.1. Adaptive allocation with increasing weights (more budget for later rounds) is optimal when the model is closer to convergence in later rounds, as the marginal utility of additional training data increases near convergence.

