Appendix B: Architecture and Design Specifications

B.1 System Architecture Overview

The complete system architecture comprises five layers:

Layer 1: Input Processing. Raw text observations are processed through a transformer encoder to produce $d_y$-dimensional embeddings, then compressed to $d_{\tilde{y}}$-dimensional representations via E-PCA.

Layer 2: Belief Tracking. The belief state is maintained via Bayesian update using the compressed observations and transition model.

Layer 3: Multi-Model Inference. Six models ($M_1$ through $M_6$) generate outputs for conversational response, risk estimation, uncertainty estimation, safety verification, escalation recommendation, and ethics check.

Layer 4: Verification and Safety. The rule-based verifier evaluates model outputs for consistency and compliance. The safety filter projects actions onto the admissible set.

Layer 5: Action Execution. The selected action is executed, and the system transitions to the next timestep.

B.2 Data Flow Specification

Input Flow:

1. Participant text arrives at edge device
2. Text is tokenized and encoded to $y_t \in \mathbb{R}^{768}$
3. E-PCA compresses to $\tilde{y}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{15}$
4. Likelihood $P(\tilde{y}_t | s)$ computed for all $s \in \mathcal{S}$
5. Belief state $b_t$ updated

Inference Flow:

1. Belief state $b_t$ passed to models $M_2$ through $M_5$
2. Text embedding $y_t$ passed to $M_1$ for response generation
3. Model outputs passed to verifier $V$
4. Verifier decision passed to safety filter
5. Safety filter selects action from admissible set
6. Action executed

Privacy Flow:

1. Edge inference consumes zero privacy budget
2. Model updates from federated training rounds consume $\epsilon_r$ per round
3. Total budget tracked via RDP accounting
4. Equity metrics computed by Ethics Board via secure aggregation

B.3 Component Specifications

B.3.1 E-PCA Component

Input: $y_t \in \mathbb{R}^{768}$
Output: $\tilde{y}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{15}$
Training: Minimize KL divergence between full and reconstructed observation distributions
Complexity: $O(768 \cdot 15) = 11,520$ operations per observation

B.3.2 Belief Tracker Component

Input: $\tilde{y}_t$, $u_{t-1}$, $b_{t-1}$
Output: $b_t \in \Delta^2$ (probability simplex in $\mathbb{R}^3$)
Update Rule: Bayesian update as specified in Section 3.4
Complexity: $O(9)$ operations per update

B.3.3 Verifier Component

Input: $M_1$ output text, $M_2$ score, $M_3$ uncertainty, $M_4$ verdict, $M_5$ recommendation, $M_6$ compliance
Output: $\{\text{accept}, \text{flag}, \text{reject}\}$
Rules: Five rules as specified in Section 5.2
Complexity: $O(1)$ rule evaluations

B.3.4 Safety Filter Component

Input: $\pi_{\text{AI}}$ output, $z_t$ system state
Output: $\pi_{\text{safe}}$ action from admissible set
Method: Projection onto admissible set as specified in Section 4.3
Complexity: $O(|\mathcal{U}|) = O(6)$ operations

B.4 Integration Protocol

Definition B.4.1 (Complete System Pipeline).

```
Algorithm: Complete System Pipeline
Input: Raw text observation text_t
Output: Action u_t

1. y_t ← TransformerEncode(text_t)          # d_y = 768
2. ỹ_t ← EPCACompress(y_t)                  # d_ỹ = 15
3. b_t ← BeliefUpdate(ỹ_t, u_{t-1}, b_{t-1})  # O(9)
4. M1_out ← M1.generate(text_t, b_t)
5. M2_out ← M2.predict(ỹ_t, b_t)
6. M3_out ← M3.uncertainty(ỹ_t, b_t)
7. M4_out ← M4.verify(M1_out)
8. M5_out ← M5.recommend(ỹ_t, b_t)
9. M6_out ← M6.check(M1_out, b_t)
10. V_decision ← Verifier(M1_out, M2_out, M3_out, M4_out, M5_out, M6_out)
11. if V_decision == reject:
12.     u_t ← u_review
13. elif V_decision == flag:
14.     u_t ← SafetyFilter(π_AI, {u_review, u_wait})
15. else:
16.     u_t ← SafetyFilter(π_AI, U_admissible)
17. return u_t
```

