Appendix F: Complete Mathematical Derivations

F.1 Belief Update with E-PCA Compression

Derivation F.1.1 (Compressed Observation Likelihood).

The compressed observation $\tilde{y}_t$ is obtained via E-PCA projection of the full observation $y_t$. The likelihood of the compressed observation given state $s$ is:

P(\tilde{y}_t | s) = \int P(\tilde{y}_t | y_t) P(y_t | s) dy_t

Assuming a deterministic E-PCA projection $\tilde{y}_t = g(y_t)$:

P(\tilde{y}_t | s) = P(g(y_t) | s) = \int \delta(\tilde{y}_t - g(y_t)) P(y_t | s) dy_t

In practice, the E-PCA projection is not perfectly deterministic, and the likelihood is approximated via softmax:

P(\tilde{y}_t | s) \approx \text{softmax}(W_s^T \tilde{y}_t + b_s)

F.2 Transition Matrix Robustness Set

Definition F.2.1 (Robustness Set Construction).

The robustness set $\mathcal{T}$ is constructed as:

\mathcal{T} = \{T \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3} : T_{ij} \geq 0, \sum_j T_{ij} = 1, T_{ii} \geq 0.5, T_{13} \leq 0.05, T_{31} \geq 0.03\}

Proposition F.2.1 (Non-emptiness). The robustness set $\mathcal{T}$ is non-empty.

Proof. The baseline transition matrix:

T_{\text{baseline}} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.95 & 0.04 & 0.01 \\ 0.10 & 0.85 & 0.05 \\ 0.05 & 0.30 & 0.65 \end{bmatrix}

satisfies all constraints: $T_{11} = 0.95 \geq 0.5$, $T_{22} = 0.85 \geq 0.5$, $T_{33} = 0.65 \geq 0.5$, $T_{13} = 0.01 \leq 0.05$, $T_{31} = 0.05 \geq 0.03$. Therefore, $T_{\text{baseline}} \in \mathcal{T}$, and $\mathcal{T} \neq \emptyset$. $\blacksquare$

F.3 Belief Entropy and Uncertainty

Definition F.3.1 (Belief Entropy).

H(b_t) = -\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} b_t(s) \log b_t(s)

Proposition F.3.1 (Entropy Bounds). For $|\mathcal{S}| = 3$:

0 \leq H(b_t) \leq \log(3) \approx 1.099

The minimum occurs at degenerate beliefs (all mass on one state), and the maximum occurs at uniform beliefs (equal mass on all states).

Theorem F.3.1 (Entropy and Uncertainty Monotonicity). If $H(b_t) \geq \tau_U$, then there is no state $s$ with $b_t(s) \geq 1 - \delta$ for $\delta < \frac{1}{2}$.

Proof. If $b_t(s) \geq 1 - \delta$ for some $s$, then:

H(b_t) \leq -(1-\delta)\log(1-\delta) - \delta\log(\delta/2)

For $\delta < \frac{1}{2}$, this bound is strictly less than $\log(3)$. Therefore, high entropy implies no state is highly probable. $\blacksquare$

F.4 Disagreement Metric Derivation

Definition F.4.1 (Correlation-Corrected Disagreement).

\Delta_t = \frac{|M_2 - M_5|}{\sqrt{1 - \rho_{25}^2}}

Derivation F.4.1 (Variance Correction).

If $M_2$ and $M_5$ are correlated with correlation $\rho_{25}$, their difference has variance:

\text{Var}(M_2 - M_5) = \text{Var}(M_2) + \text{Var}(M_5) - 2\text{Cov}(M_2, M_5)

Assuming both have unit variance:

\text{Var}(M_2 - M_5) = 2 - 2\rho_{25} = 2(1 - \rho_{25})

The standardized difference is:

\frac{|M_2 - M_5|}{\sqrt{2(1-\rho_{25})}}

The correction $\sqrt{1-\rho_{25}^2}$ in the denominator accounts for the fact that correlated errors are less informative than independent errors. When $\rho_{25} = 0$, the correction is 1. When $\rho_{25} \to 1$, the correction approaches 0, amplifying the disagreement signal.

F.5 Complete Proof of Escalation Threshold Optimality

Theorem F.5.1. For the three-action escalation problem with cost matrix $C$, the optimal policy partitions the belief simplex into three regions.

Proof. The expected cost of each action is:

\mathbb{E}[C(a_j) | b] = \sum_{k=1}^{3} c_{jk} b_k

For action $a_1$ to be optimal, it must satisfy:

\mathbb{E}[C(a_1) | b] \leq \mathbb{E}[C(a_j) | b] \quad \forall j \neq 1

This gives two linear inequalities in $b$:

(c_{11} - c_{21}) b_1 + (c_{12} - c_{22}) b_2 + (c_{13} - c_{23}) b_3 \leq 0

(c_{11} - c_{31}) b_1 + (c_{12} - c_{32}) b_2 + (c_{13} - c_{33}) b_3 \leq 0

These define half-planes in the belief simplex. The intersection of half-planes defines the region where $a_1$ is optimal. The boundaries are linear, and the regions are convex. $\blacksquare$

